TI  - The role of urban green space morphology and threshold in cooling efficiency: Evidence from five cities, China
T2  - JOURNAL OF CLEANER PRODUCTION
AB  - Urban heat islands pose significant public health risks due to elevated land surface temperatures (LSTs). Although urban green spaces (UGSs) effectively mitigate LST, enhancing cooling efficiency through optimizing UGS morphology (UGSM) remains a challenge. This study identifies UGSM types in five megacities through morphological spatial pattern analysis (MSPA) and hierarchical clustering. To identify high-efficiency cooling regions, a novel method based on multiscale geographically weighted regression (MGWR) was developed and interactively validated via random forest and XGBoost models. We found that high-efficiency cooling regions are primarily concentrated in higher LST zones (MH, H). In these regions, significant spatial non-stationary effects of UGSMs impact on the LST were observed. Compared with that of the whole region, the explanatory efficiency of UGSMs for LST variation increased by more than 15 %. In high-efficiency cooling regions, the bridge-dominated mixed UGSM (Bri-MUGSM) exhibited the highest explanatory power, explaining over 91 % of the LST variation with XGBoost. Notably, 85.72 % of the UGSM indicators (MSPs) exhibited nonlinear threshold impacts on the LST. Among all the UGSMs, for cores with values below 40 ha/km2, every 10 ha increase corresponds to an approximate 1 degrees C decrease in LST. When perforation exceeds approximately 20 ha/km2, it continues to effectively mitigate LST, while the cooling efficiency of other MSPs shows relatively typical environmental dependency due to significant differences in different UGSMs. This study provides scientific foundation for identifying high-efficiency cooling regions, thereby improving the UGS cooling efficiency and introduces a new diagnostic framework for identifying high-efficiency UGS areas to address other environmental challenges.
