TI  - Urban form and green space structure as drivers of urban heat mitigation
T2  - SUSTAINABLE CITIES AND SOCIETY
AB  - This study examines how urban form and green space structure influence urban heat mitigation, focusing specifically on land surface temperature (LST). Urban form variables-including Density, Concentration, Clustering, and Continuity-were constructed to capture building-driven heat dynamics, while green space structure was characterized by landscape metrics (Area, Shape, Aggregation) derived from Fragstats. Using Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modelling (PLS-SEM), this research identified direct and indirect relationships among these variables and their combined impact on LST. Results show that urban density alone does not directly increase temperatures; however, lower spatial concentration indirectly reduces green space effectiveness, raising LST. Conversely, compact urban development with higher spatial concentration significantly promotes heat mitigation by supporting larger, better-connected green areas. These findings emphasize the importance of spatially integrated green infrastructure in urban planning for effective thermal resilience.
