TI  - Mapping urban heat island networks using a landscape connectivity approach: A case study of Nanjing
T2  - SUSTAINABLE CITIES AND SOCIETY
AB  - High-density cities experience severe urban heat island (UHI) effects due to intensive development and limited ecological space. While previous studies have predominantly focused on patch-based mitigation strategies, the role of spatial connectivity in regulating urban thermal environments remains underexplored. This study proposes a UHI network framework grounded in landscape connectivity to establish a multi-scale thermal regulation system characterized by "node-corridor-source". The methodology integrates atmospheric correction for land surface temperature retrieval, morphological spatial pattern analysis, landscape connectivity assessment, and circuit theory. Based on these components, a three-level optimization strategy is formulated, targeting critical nodes, thermal corridors, and heat sources. The main findings are: 1) a composite resistance surface reveals significantly higher resistance values in central urban areas compared to peripheral regions; 2) 43 heat sources, 105 thermal corridors, 21 cooling nodes, and 19 heating nodes are identified in Nanjing, with first-level heat sources are highly concentrated in rapidly urbanizing districts such as Jiangning, revealing localized thermal vulnerabilities; 3) targeted interventions through a hierarchical regulation strategy effectively mitigate thermal diffusion. This study provides a scientific framework for managing the urban thermal environments and supports precise, adaptive heat mitigation strategies in high-density cities.
