AU  - Chen, YQ
TI  - Combined effects of building and tree on the diurnal thermal environment in urban functional zones from 2D and 3D perspectives
T2  - ECOLOGICAL FRONTIERS
AB  - Urban buildings and trees are not only core components of grey-green infrastructure but also key means for alleviating the urban heat island (UHI) effect via urban landscape design and management. However, due to data availability, few studies have used high-resolution remote sensing data and geographic information data to explore the combined effects of building and tree on diurnal land surface temperature (LST) in different urban functional zones (UFZs) from two- and three-dimensional perspectives. Therefore, this study employed Pearson correlation analysis and boosted regression tree (BRT) model to investigate the relationships between 2D and 3D morphology of building and tree and diurnal LST across UFZs, using Beijing in China as a case study. Results indicate that: (1) Significant differences exist in diurnal distribution of LST between various UFZs, so different objects should be focused on in the regulation of the diurnal urban thermal environment. (2) Dominant factors influencing LST and their marginal effect patterns vary and largely depend on observation times and UFZ contexts. (3) In all UFZs, the impact of 2D morphology on LST is far greater than that of 3D morphology during the day, and vice versa at night. Detailed 3D urban characteristics can effectively improve the interpretation of urban morphology for LSTs at the UFZ scale, with an average optimization level of 22 %. (4) The warming or cooling thresholds of dominant factors differ across UFZs. Furthermore, the diurnal marginal effect patterns of dominant factors in certain UFZs exhibit opposing trends. For such zones, stakeholders need to combine the threshold ranges at which the dominant factors produce cooling effects during day and night, and prudently consider the diurnal thermal comfort trade-offs based on the primary function of the specific location. These findings provide fresh insights into the impact of 3D urban features on diurnal LST and have significant implications for climate resilience planning and UHI mitigation strategies from diurnal and UFZ perspectives.
