AU  - Zhang, SY
AU  - Wang, XR
TI  - Comparing 2D and 3D green quantity and spatial patterns in Built-Up areas of 292 Chinese cities
T2  - ECOLOGICAL INDICATORS
AB  - Urban green space (UGS) is vital to ecological functioning and human well-being, yet current evaluations rely mainly on two-dimensional (2D) indicators. Three-dimensional (3D) green quantity, which measures vegetation volume, offers a more comprehensive ecological metric. However, research on 3D green quantity remains fragmented, with limited comparative assessments across cities nationwide or systematic comparison to traditional 2D indicators. This study evaluates both 2D and 3D green quantity across the built-up areas of 292 Chinese cities, using green coverage rate (GCR) and vegetation volume per unit area as respective indicators. Through spatial, equity, correlation, and city typology analyses, the research compares the scale and distribution of UGS nationwide. The results reveal that: (1) the 2D green quantity in Chinese cities ranges from 23.91 % (Kumul) to 73.79 % (Panzhihua), while the 3D green quantity varies from 0.003 m3/m2 (Nagqu) to 2.25 m3/m2 (Shiyan); (2) significant spatial disparities exist between 2D and 3D green quantity, with 2D exhibiting a more balanced distribution and 3D forming complex, polycentric clusters with marked local variation, identifying cities such as Beijing as high outliers and Bijie as a low outlier; (3) green space characteristics vary by city type, with megacities exhibiting the highest and most balanced levels, and markedly higher 3D green quantity than other city types, while small and medium-sized cities show lower values and greater disparities, and inequality is considerably more pronounced in 3D green quantity than in 2D; (4) deviation analysis further reveals that compared with 3D metrics, 2D metrics tend to underestimate the level of UGS in highly urbanized cities, highlighting structural imbalances in green space development across development stages; (5) green quantity indicators display distinct associations with environmental factors, where 2D green quantity is more strongly correlated with urban heat island intensity, while 3D green quantity shows stronger associations with PM2.5 reduction and water yield. By incorporating 3D green quantity, this study provides a complementary assessment of UGS at the national level in China, revealing differences in 2D and 3D green quantity across city types. The findings support the need for differentiated greening strategies and suggest that integrating 3D green quantity into planning can enhance ecological outcomes and urban sustainability.
