TI  - Identifying thresholds of landscape indicators in suitable green space buffer zones based on thermal sensation categories during summer: Empirical study in Chongqing, China
T2  - SUSTAINABLE CITIES AND SOCIETY
AB  - Identifying suitable buffers for green space elements and their thresholds is critical for enhancing thermal comfort but remains understudied. We established 1755 buffer zones across six urban parks in Chongqing. Physiological Equivalent Temperature (PET) and twelve landscape indicators (involving tree, building, and underlying surface elements) were evaluated for each buffer using numerical simulations, field measurements, ArcGIS, and UAV-based 3D models. The relationship between indicators and PET were analyzed to identify suitable green space buffer zones (SGBs) for different elements. Indicator interaction effects and thresholds were explored within the integrated SGB. Results showed that SGB radii varied: 10-25 m for trees, 20-50 m for buildings, and 25-50 m for underlying surfaces, with an integrated SGB radius of 25 m. Indicators related to horizontal structure (tree canopy coverage [TC] and stone pavement area ratio) and vertical structure (tree species richness) exhibited interactive effects on PET, with significant temporal variation. Recommended thresholds for "slightly warm" and "neutral" thermal sensation categories were TC > 0.43, average tree height > 10.1 m, average branching position height > 3.6 m, 3-7 tree species, average building height <= 6 m, and shrubgrass coverage ratio > 0.64. This study provides practical references for optimizing thermal comfort in green spaces.
