AU  - Kong, LQ
AU  - Cao, Q
AU  - Liu, LM
AU  - Zhou, R
AU  - Du, SQ
TI  - Who are marginalized? Unequal distribution of urban street shading in Shanghai
T2  - BUILDING AND ENVIRONMENT
AB  - Urban street shading is a proven, cost-effective strategy for reducing pedestrian exposure to extreme heat, yet its equitable distribution across social groups in dense metropolitan areas remains understudied. This research evaluates the distributional justice of street shading (encompassing both tree shading and artificial shading) in Shanghai's central urban area by combining 163,571 Baidu Street View panoramas, deep learning-based semantic segmentation, building height-based simulation model, and census data from 2408 residential communities. We found that: (1) Both natural and artificial shading are heavily concentrated in the urban core; (2) Children, non-local residents, individuals with lower educational attainment, and low-income groups experience significant shading deficits; (3) Buffer areas between developed and developing zones face dual challenges of insufficient and unequal shading coverage; (4) Nearly 47.8 % of residential walking spaces require additional street tree planting, while 13.2 % could be rapidly remediated with artificial solutions such as awnings or umbrellas. We advocate a hybrid shading strategy that combines rapid deployment of artificial structures with long-term investments in street trees to maximize cooling benefits and advance climate justice. These insights can help policymakers better target shading infrastructure efforts and alleviate heat-related health disparities in urban communities.
