AU  - Zhao, CH
AU  - Li, YR
TI  - How does vegetation influence surface temperature across various road types and urban morphology types?
T2  - BUILDING AND ENVIRONMENT
AB  - Green vegetation plays a role in cooling and alleviating the urban thermal environment. However, few studies have explored how the relationship between vegetation and Land Surface Temperature (LST) changed across various road types and Urban Morphology Types (UMTs). In this study, we utilize Google Street View's 360 degrees panoramic images and deep learning to calculate Green View Index (GVI) and retrieve LST via Landsat 8 TIRS/ OLI remote sensing images in the Austin metropolitan area in Texas, US. Then, we analyze the correlation between GVI and LST under different UMTs and road types. The results indicate: 1) different UMTs and road types in the city exhibit varying GVI and LST; 2) there exists a significant negative correlation between GVI and LST, which is influenced by UMTs but to varying degrees; 3) tertiary road types show the most significant cooling effect, due to the characteristics of the roads and the design of vegetation structures. These results can serve as an important reference for theoretical research and practical applications in the fields of climate-adaptive urban planning and green infrastructure planning.
