TI  - Assessing spatial inequities of thermal environment and blue-green intervention for vulnerable populations in dense urban areas
T2  - URBAN CLIMATE
AB  - In the context of climate change and urbanization, equitable development in high-density cities faces significant challenges, particularly regarding thermal environments. This study utilizes satellite-derived land surface temperature (LST) to characterize thermal conditions. It employs the Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI) and the Modified Normalized Difference Water Index (MNDWI) to represent vegetation and water cover. It examines the issue of unequal distribution of burdens and benefits related to urban thermal environments from the perspective of vulnerable populations (e.g., age, ethnicity, income). Utilizing Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) linear regression and Geographically Weighted Regression (GWR) models, the study reveals statistical associations between LST, NDVI, and MNDWI and various vulnerable groups, as well as the spatial heterogeneity of thermal environment inequity. The findings indicate that blue-green spaces exhibit a cooling effect, while urban green spaces show a significantly greater reduction in LST than water. Vulnerable groups experience inequities in both enduring thermal environments and accessing blue-green resources. For instance, disadvantaged groups such as ethnic minorities and children often live in streets exposed to severe thermal conditions. Seasonal variations and spatial heterogeneity mark these disparities. Moreover, in rapidly developing high-density cities, the inequities faced by certain vulnerable groups contradict findings from previous studies. These findings underscore the necessity of addressing the disproportionate thermal burdens faced by vulnerable populations and emphasize the importance of accurately identifying and locating areas of thermal inequity in future policy planning.
