TI  - Intensification of land surface temperature and its seasonal variation in rapidly growing municipalities of Kolkata metropolitan area
T2  - SUSTAINABLE CITIES AND SOCIETY
AB  - This study investigates the seasonal dynamicity of Land Surface Temperature (LST) in municipalities having the most significant urbanisation rate in the 21st century within the Kolkata Metropolitan Area (KMA). It analyses the seasonal efficiency of mediating LST by green and blue spaces and the relative role of local factors concerning built up properties. The cooling efficiency is the specific difference between the mean LST of green and blue spaces from that of the respective municipal clusters, relative to the latter. Alongside the summer, winter LST distribution also has invariably more intense clustering of outliers in 2022 compared to 2001 and 2011, throughout the municipalities but growth of summer LST is more robust with lower entropy. Dominance of edge expansion of above long-term average LST patches (2001-2022) with time is evident and occurred probably due to the common phenomenon of node-centric urban growth. The average growth of summer and winter LST in 2022, relative to 2001, was 36.84 % and 31.19 % respectively, while the same for blue and green space in summer was 35.01 % and 33.98 % respectively and the same in winter were 28.73 % and 30.75 % respectively. The blue spaces have greater LST mediating efficiency in winter while the green spaces have in summer. The Variable Importance for Projection (VIP) scores portrayed number of floors as most dominant local controlling factors of LST growth probably due to the blocking of air drainage by unplanned high-rises. Apart from its significance in urban planning, the findings could be utilised in real estate business ecology for estimating land value.
